hadiths[50]{hadithnumber,text}:
"146401","Malik related to me from Nafi from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah"
"146402","Malik related to me from Nafi from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah"
"146501","Malik related to me from Sa'id ibn Amr ibn Shurahbil ibn Sa'id ibn Sa'd ibn..."
"146502","[1465] Malik related to me from Sa'id ibn Amr ibn Shurahbil ibn Sa'id ibn Sa'd ibn Ubada, from his father, from his grandfather, that he said: ""Sa'd ibn Ubada went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his expeditions. His mother died in Medina. It was said to her: 'Make a bequest.' She said: 'In what should I bequeath? The wealth belongs to Sa'd.' She died before Sa'd arrived. When Sa'd ibn Ubada came, that was mentioned to him. Sa'd said: 'O Messenger of Allah, will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Yes.' Sa'd said: 'The garden of such-and-such is charity on her behalf,' naming a specific garden."" Muwatta of Imam Malik, Hadith: 1465, Arabic Hadith."
"146601","Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that a man in the time of Aban ibn Uthman's amirate freed all of his slaves and did not have other property than them. Aban ibn Uthman took charge of the slaves and they were divided into three groups. Then he drew lots on the basis that which ever group drew the dead man's arrow would be free. The arrow fell to one of the thirds"
"146602","Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that a man in the time of Aban ibn Uthman's amirate freed all of his slaves and did not have other property than them. Aban ibn Uthman took charge of the slaves and they were divided into three groups. Then he drew lots on the basis that which ever group drew the dead man's arrow would be free. The arrow fell to one of the thirds"
"146701","And Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Sulayman ibn Yasar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to send Abdullah ibn Rawaha to Khaybar to estimate (yakhruṣ) between him and the Jews of Khaybar. He said: ""They collected for him some jewelry from their women's jewelry and said: 'This is yours; be lenient with us and overlook in the division.' Abdullah ibn Rawaha said: 'O Jews, by Allah, you are among the most hateful of Allah's creation to me, and that does not make me unjust to you. As for this bribe you offer, it is illicit gain (suht), and we do not eat it.' They said: 'By this the heavens and the earth stand.'"" Malik said: ""If a man enters into a musaqah (sharecropping) agreement for palm trees in which there is white land (al-bayad - uncultivated land), whatever the entrant cultivates in the white land is his. And if the landowner stipulates that he will cultivate the white land for himself, that is not valid, because the entrant irrigates for the landowner, so that is an increase he has gained over him. And if they stipulate cultivation between them, there is no harm in that if all the expenses are on the entrant: the seed, irrigation, and cultivation - all of it. But if the entrant stipulates on the landowner that the seed is on you, that is not permissible, because he has stipulated an increase on the landowner. The musaqah is only on the basis that all expenses and outlays are on the entrant, and nothing is on the landowner. This is the known form of musaqah."" Malik said: ""Concerning a spring shared by two men, its water stops flowing. One wants to work on the spring and the other says: 'I do not find what to spend on it.' It is said to the one who wants to work: 'Work and spend, and all the water is yours to irrigate with until your partner brings half of what you spent.' When he brings half of what you spent, he takes his share of the water. The first is given all the water only because he spent. And if he achieved nothing by his work, the other is not liable for any of the expenses."" Malik said: ""And if all the expenses and outlays are on the garden owner, and nothing is on the entrant except that he works with his hand, he is merely a hireling for a portion of the fruit. That is not valid, because he does not know how much his hire is if nothing specific is named for him to know and work for. He does not know whether that will be little or much."" Malik said: ""And every muqarid (capital investor) or musaqi (sharecropper) should not stipulate to take something from the capital or from the palm trees less than his partner. Because that makes him a hireling for that: he says 'I will sharecrop for you on condition you work for me in such-and-such palms, irrigating and pollinating them, and I will invest such-and-such capital on condition you work for me for ten dinars not part of what I invest.' That is not permissible nor valid, and that is the ruling among us."" Malik said: ""The Sunnah in musaqah which the garden owner may stipulate on the sharecropper is: repairing the walls, cleaning the spring, canal irrigation, pollinating the palms, cutting the fronds, and harvesting the fruit - this and its like, on condition the sharecropper gets half the fruit, or less, or more, if they agree. But the landowner may not stipulate new work the worker initiates, such as a well he digs, or a spring he raises, or trees he plants, bringing the root from his own property, or a waterwheel he builds whose expense increases. That is like saying to a man: 'Build me a house here, or dig me a well, or dig a well, or dig me a spring, or do me work for half the fruit of this my garden before the fruit ripens and its sale becomes lawful.' This is selling fruit before its soundness appears, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling fruits until their soundness appears."" Malik said: ""But if the fruit has ripened and its soundness appears and its sale becomes lawful, then a man says to another: 'Do some of these works for me' - for a work he names - 'for half the fruit of this my garden,' there is no harm in that. He has only hired him for something known and seen, which he has seen and approved. But as for musaqah: if the garden has no fruit, or its fruit is little, or it spoils, he gets only that. And a hireling is only hired for something named; hire is not valid except with that. Hire is a sale among sales; he only buys his work from him. And that is not valid if it contains gharar, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of gharar."" Malik said: ""The Sunnah in musaqah among us is that it is in the root of every palm, vine, olive, pomegranate, peach, or what resembles those roots - permissible, no harm in it, on condition the landowner gets half the fruit from that, or a third, or a quarter, or more, or less."" Malik said: ""And musaqah is also permissible in crops if they have emerged and become independent, and the owner is unable to irrigate, work, and tend them. Musaqah in that is also permissible."" Malik said: ""Musaqah is not valid in anything of the roots in which musaqah is permissible if there is fruit that has ripened, its soundness appears, and its sale becomes lawful. It should only be sharecropped from the coming year. And musaqah of what has become lawful to sell of fruits is hire, because he only sharecrops the owner of the root for fruit whose soundness has appeared, on condition he suffices him and harvests it for him, like giving him dinars and dirhams. That is not musaqah. Muaqah is only from when the palm is harvested until the fruit ripens and its sale becomes lawful."" Malik said: ""And whoever sharecrops fruit in a root before its soundness appears and its sale becomes lawful, that musaqah is valid."" Malik said: ""And white land should not be sharecropped. That is because its owner may lawfully rent it for dinars, dirhams, and similar known prices. But as for the man who gives his white land for a third or a quarter of what comes out of it, that enters gharar, because crops vary - sometimes little, sometimes much, and perhaps they perish entirely. So the landowner leaves a known rent that would be valid for him to rent his land for, and takes something uncertain, not knowing if it will be fulfilled or not. This is disliked. And that is like a man who hires a worker for a journey for a known wage, then the hirer says: 'Would you like me to give you a tenth of what I profit in this journey as your hire?' That is not lawful nor permissible."" Malik said: ""And a man should not hire himself out, nor his land, nor his ship, except for something known that does not change to something else."" Malik said: ""The distinction between musaqah in palms and white land is that the palm owner cannot sell its fruit until its soundness appears, while the landowner rents it out while it is white land with nothing in it."" Malik said: ""And the ruling among us for palms is also that they may be sharecropped for three years, four years, less, or more."" He said: ""That is what I heard, and everything like that of the roots is like palms: whatever years are permissible in palms is permissible in them."" Malik said: ""The sharecropper does not take from the one who sharecropped him anything of gold, silver, food, or anything - no increase. That is not valid nor permissible. The sharecropper should not take from the garden owner anything that increases him - not gold, nor silver, nor food, nor anything. Increase between them is not valid."" Malik said: ""And the muqarid is also in this position: it is not valid if increase enters musaqah or muqarada, for it becomes hire. And what hire enters, it is not valid nor permissible. Hire should not occur in a matter of gharar where it is not known if it will be or not, or if it will be little or much."" Malik said: ""Concerning a man who sharecrops land in which there are palms and vines or similar roots, and in it there is white land."" Malik said: ""If the white land is subordinate to the root, and the root is the greater part or most of it, there is no harm in sharecropping it. That is when the palms are two-thirds or more, and the white land is a third or less. Then the white land is subordinate to the root. But if the white land has palms or vines or similar roots, and the root is a third or less, and the white land is two-thirds or more, rent is permissible in that, and musaqah is forbidden. That is because people's practice is to sharecrop the root in which there is white land, and rent the land in which there is a small amount of root. Or sell the mushaf or the sword in which there is silver ornamentation for silver, or the necklace or ring in which there are gemstones and gold for dinars. These sales have never ceased to be permissible; people trade them and buy them, and nothing has come in that described as a fixed limit: if it reaches it, it is haram; if it falls short, it is halal. The ruling in that among us is what people have acted upon and permitted among themselves: that if the silver or gold in that is subordinate to what it is in, its sale is permissible. That is when the blade, or the mushaf, or the gemstones are two-thirds of the value or more, and the ornamentation is a third or less."""""""
"146702","Mālik narrated to me that it reached him that a man from the Anṣār, from the Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, gave charity to his parents. Then they died, and their son inherited the wealth, which was date palms. He asked the Messenger of Allāh, peace be upon him, about that, and he said: \You have been rewarded for your charity"
"146801","Yahya said: Malik said: ""The best I have heard concerning workers (slaves) in musaqah, which the sharecropper stipulates on the landowner, is that there is no harm in it, because they are workers of the capital, so they are like the capital. There is no benefit in them for the entrant except that the expense is lightened by them. If they were not in the capital, his expense would be severe. And that is like musaqah in the spring and irrigation. You will not find anyone sharecropping two lands equal in root and benefit, one with a flowing spring and the other with irrigation, on the same terms, due to the lightness of the spring's expense and the severity of irrigation's expense."" He said: ""And that is the ruling among us."" He said: ""The flowing spring (al-wathina) is the one whose water is established, which does not sink nor cease."" Malik said: ""It is not for the sharecropper to work the capital's workers in other than it, nor to stipulate that on the one who sharecropped him."" Malik said: ""And it is not permissible for the one who sharecropped to stipulate on the capital owner slaves to work in the garden who were not in it when he sharecropped it."" Malik said: ""And the capital owner should not stipulate on the one who entered his capital by musaqah that he takes one of the capital's slaves and removes him from the capital. Muaqah of the capital is only on the condition it is in."" He said: ""So if the capital owner wants to remove one of the capital's slaves, let him remove him before the musaqah. Or if he wants to introduce one, let him do that before the musaqah, then sharecrop after that if he wishes."" He said: ""And whoever dies of the slaves, or is absent, or falls ill, the capital owner must replace him."""""""
"146802","Malik related to me from Nafi from Abdullah ibn Umar that Umar ibn al-Khattab said"
"146901","Malik related to me that he had heard that a slave-girl came to Umar ibn al-Khattab (who had been beaten by her master with a red hot iron) and he set her free. Malik said"
"146902","Malik related to me that he had heard that a slave-girl came to Umar ibn al-Khattab (who had been beaten by her master with a red hot iron) and he set her free. Malik said"
"147001","Malik related to me from Hilal ibn Usama from Ata ibn Yasar that Umar ibn al-Hakam said"
"147002","Malik related to me from Hilal ibn Usama from Ata ibn Yasar that Umar ibn al-Hakam said"
"147101","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud that one of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah"
"147102","Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud that one of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah"
"147201","Malik related to me that he had heard that al-Maqburi said that Abu Hurayra was asked whether a man who had to free a slave"
"147202","Malik related to me that he had heard that al-Maqburi said that Abu Hurayra was asked whether a man who had to free a slave"
"147301","Malik related to me that he had heard that Fadala ibn Ubayd al- Ansari who was one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah"
"147302","Malik related to me that he had heard that Fadala ibn Ubayd al- Ansari who was one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah"
"147401","Malik related to me that he had heard that Abdullah ibn Umar was asked whether a slave could be bought on the specific condition that it was to be used to fulfil the obligation of freeing a slave"
"147402","Malik related to me that he had heard that Abdullah ibn Umar was asked whether a slave could be bought on the specific condition that it was to be used to fulfil the obligation of freeing a slave"
"147501","Malik related to me from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abi Amra al-Ansari that his mother had wanted to make a bequest"
"147502","Malik related to me from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abi Amra al-Ansari that his mother had wanted to make a bequest"
"147601","Mālik related that it reached him that Sa‘īd ibn al-Musayyab was asked about the pre-emption right, whether there was any Sunnah concerning it. He said: Yes, the pre-emption right is in houses and lands, and it only occurs between partners."
"147602","Mālik related to me, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sa‘īd ibn al-Musayyab, that ‘Uthmān ibn ‘Affān said: Whoever grants a gift to his young child who has not yet reached the age of taking possession of his gift, and he announces this and witnesses it, then it is valid, even if his father manages it for him."
"147701","And Malik related to me that it reached him from Sulayman ibn Yasar, the like of that. Malik said: ""Concerning a man who buys a share with people in land with livestock - a slave or slave girl or similar goods - then the partner comes to take by his pre-emption (shuf'a) after that, and finds the slave or slave girl has perished, and no one knew their value. The buyer says: 'The value of the slave or slave girl is a hundred dinars.' The pre-emptor partner says: 'Rather, their value is fifty dinars.'"" Malik said: ""The buyer swears that the value of what he bought with is a hundred dinars. Then if the pre-emptor wishes to take, he takes, or leaves - unless the pre-emptor brings proof that the value of the slave or slave girl is less than what the buyer said."" Malik said: ""Whoever gifts a share in a house or shared land, and the gifted one rewards him for it with cash or goods, the partners may take it by pre-emption if they wish, and they pay the gifted one the value of his reward in dinars or dirhams."" Malik said: ""Whoever gifts a gift in a shared house or land, and is not rewarded for it nor asked for it, and his partner wants to take it by its value, that is not for him unless he is rewarded for it. If he is rewarded, it is for the pre-emptor by the value of the reward."" Malik said: ""Concerning a man who buys a share in shared land for a price to a term, and the partner wants to take it by pre-emption."" Malik said: ""If he is solvent, he has pre-emption at that price to that term. And if he is feared not to pay the price to that term, if he brings a solvent, trustworthy guarantor like the one who bought the share from him in the shared land, that is for him."" Malik said: ""The absence of the absent does not cut off his pre-emption, even if his absence is prolonged. And there is no limit for us at which pre-emption is cut off."" Malik said: ""Concerning a man who bequeaths land to a group of his children, then a child is born to one of the group, then the father dies. One of the deceased's children sells his right in that land. The seller's brother is more entitled to pre-emption than his uncles, partners of his father."" Malik said: ""And this is the ruling among us."" Malik said: ""Pre-emption among partners is according to their shares. Each takes by pre-emption according to his share, if little then little, if much then according to it. That is if they dispute over it."" Malik said: ""But if a man buys from a man among his partners his right, and one of the partners says: 'I will take from pre-emption according to my share.' And the buyer says: 'If you wish to take all the pre-emption, I will hand it to you; if you wish to leave it, leave it.' If the buyer gives him this choice and hands it to him, the pre-emptor has only to take all the pre-emption or hand it to him. If he takes it, he is more entitled to it; otherwise, he has nothing."" Malik said: ""Concerning a man who buys land, then develops it with roots he places in it, or a well he digs, then a man comes and establishes a right in it, and wants to take it by pre-emption: he has no pre-emption in it unless he gives him the value of what he developed. If he gives him the value of what he developed, he is more entitled to pre-emption; otherwise, he has no right in it."" Malik said: ""Whoever sells his share of shared land or house, then when he knows the pre-emptor will take by pre-emption, the buyer seeks rescission (istaqala) and the seller rescinds (aqalah). That is not for him; the pre-emptor is more entitled to it at the price he sold it for."" Malik said: ""Whoever buys a share in a house or land, and livestock and goods, in a single transaction, and the pre-emptor demands his pre-emption in the house or land. The buyer says: 'Take all I bought, for I only bought it all together.'"" Malik said: ""Rather, the pre-emptor takes his pre-emption in the house or land by its share of that price. Each thing he bought is set up separately at the price he bought it for. Then the pre-emptor takes his pre-emption by what falls to it of the value from the total price. And he does not take anything of the livestock and goods unless he wishes that."" Malik said: ""And whoever sells a share of shared land, and some of those with pre-emption in it surrender to the seller, and some refuse except to take by their pre-emption: whoever refuses to surrender takes all the pre-emption, and it is not for him to take according to his right and leave the rest."" Malik said: ""Concerning a group of partners in one house. One sells his share, and his partners are all absent except one. The present one is offered to take by pre-emption or leave. He says: 'I will take my share and leave my partners' shares until they come. If they take, that is it; if they leave, I will take all the pre-emption.'"" Malik said: ""He has only to take all of it or leave it. If his partners come, they take from him or leave if they wish. So when this is offered to him and he does not accept it, I do not see him having pre-emption."""""""
"147702","Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from A'isha"
"147801","Yahya said: Malik said from Muhammad ibn Umarah from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm that Uthman ibn Affan said: ""When boundaries fall in the land, there is no pre-emption in it, nor in a well, nor in a palm pollinator (fahl al-nakhl)."" Malik said: ""And on this is the ruling among us."" Malik said: ""And there is no pre-emption in a road, whether division is valid in it or not."" Malik said: ""And the ruling among us is that there is no pre-emption in a house courtyard (arṣat dar), whether division is valid in it or not."" Malik said: ""Concerning a man who buys a share of shared land on condition that he has the option (khiyar) in it, and the seller's partners want to take what their partner sold by pre-emption before the buyer chooses: that is not for them until the buyer takes and the sale is established for him. When the sale becomes binding for him, they have pre-emption."" And Malik said: ""Concerning a man who buys land, it remains in his hands for a while, then a man comes and establishes a right in it by inheritance: he has pre-emption if his right is established. And whatever the land produces of produce is for the first buyer until the day the other's right is established, because he had guaranteed it - if what was in it of plantings perished or a flood took it."" He said: ""If the time is prolonged, or witnesses perish, or the seller or buyer dies, or both are alive but the origin of the sale and purchase is forgotten due to the length of time, then pre-emption is cut off, and he takes his established right. But if his matter is otherwise than this, in recent and near time, and he sees that the seller concealed the price and hid it to cut off the pre-emptor's right by that, the land is valued at what is seen to be its price, so its price becomes that. Then what increased in the land of building, planting, or development is looked at, and it is on the basis of what is on one who bought land for a known price, then built and planted in it, then the pre-emptor took it after that."" Malik said: ""And pre-emption is established in the dead's wealth as it is in the living's wealth. If the dead's heirs fear the dead's wealth will be broken up by division, they divide it then sell it; there is no pre-emption on them in it."" Malik said: ""And there is no pre-emption among us in a slave, nor slave girl, nor camel, nor cow, nor sheep, nor in anything of livestock, nor in a garment, nor in a well that has no white land. Pre-emption is only in what is divisible and in which boundaries fall, of land. As for what division is not valid in, there is no pre-emption in it."" Malik said: ""And whoever buys land in which there is pre-emption for people present, let him refer them to the ruler. Either they establish their right, or the ruler hands it to him. If he leaves them and does not refer their matter to the ruler, and they knew of his purchase, then they left that until the time was prolonged, then they came demanding their pre-emption: I do not see that for them."""""""
"147802","Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar freed an illegitimate child and its mother"
