"148102","Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Amra bint Abd ar- Rahman that Barira came asking the help of A'isha"
"148201","Malik related to me from Abdullah ibn Dinar from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah"
"148202","Malik related to me from Abdullah ibn Dinar from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah"
"148301","Malik said that it was not permissible for a slave to buy himself from his master on the provision that he could give the wala' to whomever he wished as the wala' was for the one who set him free"
"148302","Malik said that it was not permissible for a slave to buy himself from his master on the provision that he could give the wala' to whomever he wished as the wala' was for the one who set him free"
"148401","Malik related to me that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab was asked who had the wala' of the children whom a slave had by a free woman. Said said"
"148402","Malik related to me that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab was asked who had the wala' of the children whom a slave had by a free woman. Said said"
"148501","Malik related to me from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn al- Harith ibn Hisham that his father told him that al-Asi ibn Hisham had died and left three sons"
"148502","Malik related to me from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn al- Harith ibn Hisham that his father told him that al-Asi ibn Hisham had died and left three sons"
"148601","Malik related to me from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm that his father told him that he was sitting with Aban ibn Uthman"
"148602","Malik related to me from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm that his father told him that he was sitting with Aban ibn Uthman"
"148701","Malik related to me that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab spoke about a man who died and left three sons and left mawali whom he had freed. Then two of his sons died and left children. He said"
"148702","Malik related to me that he had heard that Said ibn al-Musayyab spoke about a man who died and left three sons and left mawali whom he had freed. Then two of his sons died and left children. He said"
"148801","And Malik related to me that it reached him that Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar were asked: ""Is judgment given by oath with a witness?"" They said: ""Yes."" Malik said: ""The Sunnah has passed in judgment by oath with a single witness. The right-holder swears with his witness and establishes his right. If he refuses and declines to swear, the defendant is made to swear. If he swears, that right drops from him. If he refuses to swear, the right is established against him for the claimant."" Malik said: ""And that is only in property matters specifically. It does not occur in any of the hudud (prescribed punishments), nor in marriage, nor in divorce, nor in manumission, nor in theft, nor in slander (qadhf). If someone says: 'But manumission is from property matters.' He has erred. That is not as he said. If it were as he said, the slave would swear with his witness when he brings a witness that his master manumitted him. And the slave, if he brings a witness on property he claims, swears with his witness and establishes his right as the free man swears."" Malik said: ""So the Sunnah among us is that the slave, when he brings a witness on his manumission, his master is made to swear: 'I did not manumit him,' and that is nullified from him."" Malik said: ""And likewise the Sunnah among us also in divorce: if a woman brings a witness that her husband divorced her, her husband is made to swear: 'I did not divorce her.' If he swears, divorce does not fall on him."" Malik said: ""So the Sunnah of divorce and manumission in a single witness is one. The oath is only on the woman's husband and the slave's master. And manumission is a hudud (prescribed punishment) in which women's testimony is not permissible. Because when the slave is manumitted, his inviolability is established, hudud fall for him and on him. If he commits zina while muhsan, he is stoned. If he kills a slave, he is killed for him. And inheritance is established between him and those who inherit him. If a disputant argues: 'Suppose a man manumits his slave, and a man comes demanding a debt from the slave's master. A man and two women testify for him on his right. That would establish the right on the slave's master until his manumission is returned by it, if the master has no other wealth to satisfy it with. He wants to thereby permit women's testimony in manumission.' That is not as he said. The likeness of that is the man who manumits his slave, then the claimant comes to the master with a single witness. He swears with his witness, then establishes his right, and the slave's manumission is returned by that. Or a man comes who had dealings and involvement with the slave's master, and claims he has a debt on the master. It is said to the master: 'Swear you do not owe what he claims.' If he refuses and declines to swear, the claimant is sworn, and his right is established on the master. That would return the slave's manumission if the debt is established on his master."" He said: ""And likewise also the man who marries a slave girl, so she becomes his wife. The slave girl's master comes to the man who married her and says: 'You and so-and-so bought my slave girl so-and-so for such-and-such dinars.' The slave girl's husband denies that. The master brings a man and two women who testify to what he said. His sale is established, his right is confirmed, the slave girl becomes haram on her husband, and that is a separation between them. Women's testimony is not permissible in divorce."" Malik said: ""And from that also: the man who slanders a free man, so the hadd falls on him. A man and two women come and testify that the one slandered is an owned slave. That drops the hadd from the slanderer after it fell on him. Women's testimony is not permissible in slander."" Malik said: ""And from what resembles that, in which judgment differs and the passed Sunnah: two women testify to a newborn's live birth (istihlal), so his inheritance becomes obligatory by that, until he inherits and his wealth is for those who inherit him if the newborn dies. And with the two women who testified, there is no man, nor oath. And that may be in great wealth of gold, silver, real estate, gardens, slaves, and other wealth. But if two women testify to a single dirham, or less, or more, their testimony does not establish anything and is not permissible unless there is with them a witness or an oath."" Malik said: ""And among people are those who say: 'The oath is not with a single witness.' And they argue with Allah's saying, Blessed and Exalted, and His word is the Truth: {And call to witness two witnesses from your men. If they are not two men, then a man and two women from those you approve as witnesses...} [2:282]. They say: 'If he does not bring a man and two women, he has nothing, and he is not made to swear with his witness.'"" Malik said: ""So from the argument against one who says that saying, it should be said to him: 'What do you think? If a man claims a debt on a man, does not the defendant swear that that right is not on him? If he swears, it drops from him. If he refuses the oath, the claimant is sworn: ""My right is truly a right."" And his right is established on his opponent. This is what there is no difference in among anyone of the people, nor in any land. By what did he take this? Or in what place of Allah's Book did he find it? If he acknowledges this, let him acknowledge the oath with the witness. And if that is not in Allah's Book, Mighty and Majestic, it suffices from that what passed of the Sunnah. But a person may love to know the aspect of correctness and the site of the argument. In this is clarification of what was problematic of that, if Allah wills.'"""""""
"148802","Malik related to me that he had asked Ibn Shihab about a slave who was released. He said"
